<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">British Kaffraria</h1><p class="st-meta">1847 CE – 1866 CE · capital King William's Town</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A British territory carved from Xhosa land on the Eastern Cape frontier, scene of the Xhosa cattle-killing of 1856-1857.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the War of the Axe of 1846-1847, Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-harry-smith-1st-baronet">Sir Harry Smith</a> annexed the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keiskamma-river">Keiskamma</a> and Kei rivers in December 1847, reviving the abandoned <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-queen-adelaide">Province of Queen Adelaide</a>. Governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-williams-town">King William's Town</a>, it was a separate dependency under the Cape governor rather than part of the colony.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>British magistrates gradually took power from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a> chiefs, and a further war in 1850-1853 tightened control. In 1856-1857 a prophecy by the girl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nongqawuse">Nongqawuse</a> led many Xhosa to kill their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and destroy their grain; tens of thousands died of hunger. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grey">Sir George Grey</a> used the catastrophe to push survivors into wage labour and to settle German military veterans and immigrants on confiscated land.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>British Kaffraria became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a> in 1860 but could not pay its way, and it was annexed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> in 1866. Towns such as East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stutterheim">Stutterheim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> preserve the marks of its settlement schemes.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-kaffraria.jpg" alt="British Kaffraria" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: I created the map, based partly on the source map: Eastern frontier of the Colony of the Cape of Good Hope from Algoa Bay to the … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Frontier,_Cape_of_Good_Hope,_ca_1835.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1847 CE – 1866 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>King William's Town</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sir Harry Smith, Sir George Grey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Xhosa traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>isiXhosa, English, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kaffraria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=british-kaffraria" aria-label="British Kaffraria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5382 3372 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Kaffraria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5702 3628L4997 651M5702 3628L5470 3663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M5470 3663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5702 3628h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5702 3628h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=british-kaffraria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
